-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 0491 - THE QUOTE IS ON HER COURSE  (no switch; a fault fix: the board's miles become the miles the SAIL settles on)
-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- THE WORK: docs/dev-log/2026-11-24-quote-on-her-course.md; harness scripts/ops/harness/quote-on-her-course/ (before-0491.sql,
--   after-0491.sql, rollback-0491.sql); proof 194 (scripts/db/proofs/194_the_quote_is_on_her_course.sql); proof 63 sails the stored course.
--
-- THE FAULT (main's proof 63 red, confirmed on prod read only, 2026-11-24): the trade board and the trip quote (world.trip_cost,
--   voyage.reach_passage, world.board_*) read a leg's miles from public.sea_reaches / public.sea_reach_matrix; the SAIL (cmd.do_sail,
--   voyage.depart) settles on the course it carries: the sum of voyage.segments_from_course, pieces of at most 500 nm along the straight
--   lat/lon line between the course's points, each rounded to 2 decimals (the line the plotter, the no-land audit and the map draw). The
--   table was measured another way (0442: great circles between the points, scripts/ops/harness/seas-memo/measure-reaches.mjs). On the
--   32,128 stored courses the two differ by more than 0.1 % on 19,428 (median +0.19 %, p99 +6.57 %; the largest SYD-TDC +21.7 %, in
--   miles ARI-AYU +1,426.8; 2,088 pairs were quoted longer, at most BND-MNG -68.5 nm, -1.8 %); at 15 kn the quote counts fewer whole days
--   than the sail settles on 4,556 pairs, two or more fewer on 595 (5 kn: 9,578; 10 kn: 6,023; 17.5 kn: 4,014). So a trip's wages and
--   stores were quoted short (proof 63: wages 160 owed, 140 quoted).
--
-- WHAT (the lead's decision "A whole", decided by default; the owner may reverse it): ONE DISTANCE.
--   1. THE TABLE TAKES THE SAIL'S MEASURE. Every harbour pair with a stored course (public.sea_courses, 0482: 32,128 of the 32,131 pairs;
--      the other 3 share one roadstead and keep their figure) gets, both ways, the sum of voyage.segments_from_course(public.sea_course(a, b)).
--      The seed below was written by scripts/db/gen-0491-sea-lengths.mjs, which CALLS those two live functions on the chain's world (both
--      ways; equal for every pair, so 0442's law, one figure both ways, holds). It is not a second measure: the measure is the SAIL's own
--      function, run offline because 64,256 calls cost 213 s on PGlite (each also reads the raster for shelter) and must not run on prod.
--      The matrix follows through its trigger (public.sea_reach_matrix_keep, 0482). No course, raster, roadstead or sea-place cell moves.
--   2. THE PRICES FOLLOW: public.port_goods.haul_nm / haul (0173) are derived from these distances by world.haul_rows(); refilled in ONE
--      statement, as 0442 and 0446 did, and held equal to it.
--   3. THE COURSE A JOB SAILS (public.fleet_job_course, two hunks): lit (sea_courses_enabled), the stored course FIRST, the one the board's
--      miles are now measured on; a merchant lane, a coast course or her own voyage only where none is stored. An HQ card's press and a
--      standing job carry it (public.sale_lines -> public.fleet_job_sails -> here). Dark, as before.
--   KEEPING IT ONE (decided by default): the table is a stored copy of the SAIL's measure over the stored courses. A later write to
--   public.sea_courses must re-run the generator and write the lengths in the same migration. Proof 194 fails loudly otherwise: it pins
--   the courses' digest the lengths were measured from and the table's digest, and re-measures a sample of pairs live through
--   voyage.segments_from_course every run. Chosen over a trigger keeper: a keeper needs the piece loop cut out of voyage.segments_from_course
--   (a hot body every departure runs) and would re-measure 32,128 courses on prod inside a migration.
--
-- WHAT A PLAYER SEES: long trips quote the days the sail settles (wages and stores quoted no longer short); a far good's price rises a
--   little with the longer sea it crossed: on the chain's world 101,096 of 159,766 market rows move their haul (132,825 rows written: the
--   rest move haul_nm only), 99,494 up and 1,602 down (by at most 0.36 %): median 0.20 %, 95 in 100 under 2.43 %, the largest +12.36 %
--   (Ayutthaya), 6,531 rows by more than 2 % (most at Hobart 311, Sydney 300, Wellington 295, Auckland 294).
--   public.price_history is not rescaled (a chart steps by a fraction of a per cent, as 0442).
-- LOCKS: row writes on 254 rows of public.sea_reaches (one statement; the matrix rebuilt once by its statement trigger), about 133,000
--   rows of public.port_goods (one statement, 132,825 rows written on the chain's world), CREATE OR REPLACE of public.fleet_job_course.
--   lock_timeout 5 s.
-- TIME: 11.8 s for the whole file on the cached world (PGlite), most of it the refill and the live sample.
-- ROLLBACK (forward): scripts/ops/harness/quote-on-her-course/rollback-0491.sql: the harbour pairs' figures as they were, haul refilled
--   from them, fleet_job_course's pre-image; md5-pinned both ways (proved locally: 0491 then the rollback gives the before digests).
-- SELF-ASSERT: the world is the one the lengths were measured on (courses digest) and the table the one they replace (before digest);
--   after the write the table's digest is the seed's, every other cell unmoved, a sample re-measured live both ways equals the table, the
--   matrix is the built one, every market row's haul is world.haul_rows()'s and every other market column unmoved; the hunks once each and
--   reversing, the job course the stored one when lit.
-- VERSION: 0491 = 0490 + 1 (prod head 0489 when written; S2's 0490 deploys first).
-- budget: no table, no cron job, no autovacuum or fillfactor change; one rewrite of about 133,000 public.port_goods rows (0442: ~137,000).
-- news: one row, public.news_update('0491', ...): long trips quote their true days, far goods a little dearer.
-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

set local statement_timeout = '120s';
set local lock_timeout = '5s';

